md prints `(empty frontmatter)` for a file whose frontmatter cannot be parsed.

That is the same words it prints for `---\n---\n`, a fence that really is
empty, so a folder index cannot tell "this file declares no keys" from "this
file declares keys that md could not read". The file here holds
`title: [unclosed`, an unterminated YAML flow sequence.

The cause is in Frontmatter.parseFenced: the YAML branch is
`(try? Yams.load(yaml: rawString) as? [String: Any]) ?? [:]`, so a throwing
parse and a genuinely empty mapping both land on `[:]`. ListCommand then sees a
non-nil Frontmatter whose serializeData() returns "", and renderPlain turns that
into `(empty frontmatter)`.

Two things are wrong, and the expectation here only pins the first:

  1. md obtained NO frontmatter data from this file, so of the two strings the
     README documents, `(no frontmatter)` is the honest one. `(empty
     frontmatter)` states something about the file that is not true.
  2. The README also promises "Parse errors for individual files are reported to
     stderr and the run continues". No diagnostic is written at all. That half
     cannot be pinned here, because any such message would have to name the file
     by its absolute path, and the path lives in a randomly named scratch
     directory.

The complete fix is to keep the parse failure distinct from an empty mapping and
report it on stderr. If the fix chooses a third string rather than reusing
`(no frontmatter)`, update this expectation to match it and delete this marker.
